# a b s t r a c t

Herein, we propose a new strategy for designing new types of wide range pH-sensitive metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with double luminescent centers on UiO-66-2OH. The UiO-66-2OH has a ligands-based emission at \(530\,\textrm{n m}\) . To introduce another luminescent center, PMA (1,2,4,5-benzenetetracarboxylic acid), as the functional site, is used to substitute the initial ligand, BDC-2OH (2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid), of UiO-66-2OH. \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions, another luminescent center at \(613~\mathrm{nm}\) , are coordinated to the free carboxyl group on PMA. Finally, TTA (2-Thenoyltrifluoroacetone) is coordinated with \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions to balance the emission at \(613~\mathrm{nm}\) \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions) and \(530~\mathrm{nm}\) (BDC-2OH). For the sake of both strong emissions, we explored the loading levels of PMA. The optimized structure is \(\mathbf{Eu}(\mathbf{TTA})\mathbf{\textcircled{\alpha}}\mathbf{MUM5}\) ("MUM" is the abbreviation of "Mixed ligand UiO-66-2OH MOFs" and "5" represents the molar percentage of PMA is \(50\%\) ), which exhibits strong emission at \(530~\mathrm{nm}\) (alkaline solution) and \(613~\mathrm{nm}\) (neutral solution). Remarkably, the synthesized material has an exponential relationship \('R^{2}=0.9973\) ) over the pH range of 1.87 to 9.65 and a linear relationship \('R^{2}=0.9987\) ) when \(p\mathrm{H}=11.01–13.35\) . Further experiments have proved that \(\mathbf{Eu}(\mathbf{TTA})\mathbf{\textcircled{\alpha}}\mathbf{MUM5}\) could distinguish different amino acids. Based on that, we build an information transferring circle with two coding modes on \(\mathbf{Eu}(\mathbf{TTA})\mathbf{\textcircled{\alpha}}\mathbf{MUM5}\) using aseptic acid and arginine as coding factors.

