# 1. Introduction

Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are an important class of porous materials constructed from organic ligands and
metal-containing nodes via strong coordination bonds, which could form one, two, or three-dimensional infinite networks [1–3]. Benefiting from their exceptional porosity and high crystallinity, MOFs are rapidly growing as a star material in the last two decades and used extensively in gas separation [4,5], pollutants adsorption [6], catalysis [7,8], and chemical-sensing [9,10]. Comparing with traditional porous materials like zeolites and activated carbons, MOFs exhibit highly architectural flexibility and adjustable organic functionality, which largely widen the application of them [11]. Up to now, researchers have developed a great quantity of post-synthetic methods to endow MOFs with various functions, which can be roughly classified into the covalent postsynthetic modification and coordinative host-synthetic modification [12,13].

Taking advantage of the tunable structure and numerous postsynthetic modification methods, a series of luminescent ligands and metal ions (especially lanthanide ions) are incorporated into MOFs to build luminescent MOFs [14]. There are various kinds of luminescent modes in MOFs like ligand-based emission, metal-based emission, guest molecule emission, and the combination of the above mechanism [15]. Inherited the porosity and thermal stability of MOFs structure, luminescent MOFs shows various applications, such as optics [16], photocatalysis [17], biomedical imaging [18], thermometer [19], and chemical sensor [9].

Lanthanide functionalized MOFs (LFMs) are a very important subset of luminescent MOFs [20]. Based on the ''antenna effect'', LFMs commonly exhibits characteristic emission of lanthanide ions resulting from their 4f electronic shell [21]. It is easy to introducing lanthanide ion into MOFs [22] through post-synthetic modification such as coordination post-synthesis [23] and central ion replacement [24]. Generally, the original framework of LFMs contain free carboxyl groups, which act as functional sites to coordinate with lanthanide ions. However, it is quite difficult to fabricate LFMs with both strong fluorescence of ligands and lanthanide ions because the extra carboxyl group would cause a large blue shift of ligands' emission to invisible wavelength. Thus, the construction of strong luminescent MOFs with multiple luminescent centers is still challenging [14].

pH value, an important physicochemical parameter, is measured frequently in industrial manufacturing, environmental monitoring, disease diagnosing [25,26]. The most popular method to measure pH is using a glass pH electrode [27], which has many advantages such as quick response, precise and so on. However, the glass pH electrodes still have some disadvantages: i) the glass electrode is too large for applications like cell imaging, ii) the rigid design makes it sensitive to extraneous electrical and microwave fields [28]. In recent years, a great number of nano-sized pH sensors are developed and applied to new applications like intracellular pH sensing and imaging [19,26,29,30]. Qian [31] and coworkers used \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions doped Nano-sized UiO-67 as a fluorescent probe with a sensing region from \(\mathsf{p H}=1.06\) to 10.90. Wang [32] and co-workers developed a new type of carbon dots for fluorometric imaging of pH variation in living cells, and it has a sensing region from \(p\mathrm{H}\,=\,6\) to 10. However, these probes can't sense strongly alkaline solution well and the luminescent color is single, which made it unable to distinguish pH values through naked eye directly. Thus, fabricating new types of fluorescent probes with multiple strong luminescent centers and a wider detection range of pH values is still a hot spot of current research.

Nowadays, luminescent coding materials are widely used in information encryption [33], information storage [34], and anticounterfeiting [35]. Breaking the limitation of spectral overlap in organic dyes and depending on their highly efficient luminescent performance, quantum dots are regarded as the ideal luminescent coding materials [36]. But they are made of toxic materials such as CdS, CdSe, \(\mathsf{C s P b C l}_{3}\). The new type of luminescent coding materials

with the quality of environmentally friendly, efficient, and inexpensive are very in demand [37].

In this paper, we fabricate a pH-sensitive mixed ligand UiO-66-20H MOFs with two luminescent centers at 530 and \(613\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Scheme 1a). The strategy can be simplified into three steps (Scheme 1b): Firstly, PMA is used to create functional sites in UiO-66-20H frameworks through ligand replacement, which could be regarded as an active site for post-synthetic modification. Secondly, \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions, another luminescent center, are introduced into the frameworks by coordinating with the free carboxyl groups on PMA. Thirdly, to balance the emission of both luminescent centers, TTA acts as an antenna to further sensitize \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions as well as coordinating ligands to protect \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions from quenching by water. As shown in Scheme 1a, the constructed sensor \(\mathbf{Eu}(\mathbf{TTA})@\mathbf{MUM5}\) has two luminescent centers: \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions based red emission \((613\,\mathrm{nm})\) and ligand-based yellow-green emission \((530\,\mathrm{nm})\). Interestingly, \(\mathbf{Eu}(\mathbf{TTA})@\mathbf{MUM5}\) exhibits different luminescent characteristics in acidic and alkaline solutions. In the acidic solution and weak alkaline solution, the emission of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions will be quenched gradually and the emission color changed from red to colorless. In the strong alkaline solution, the red emission is quenched rapidly while the green emission is first enhanced then quenched gradually. As a result, the luminescent color changed from red to yellow-green. Further research reveals that \(\mathbf{Eu}(\mathbf{TTA})@\mathbf{MUM5}\) has an exponential response region with \(\mathsf{p H}=1.87–9.65\) and a linear response region with \(\mathsf{p H}=11.01–13.35\). This probe is then applied to distinguish various amino acids aqueous solution and shows linear relationships with aspartic acid and arginine. Taking advantage of the different luminescent response in aspartic acid and arginine aqueous solution, they are used as coding factors to construct an information transferring circle with two coding modes on \(\mathbf{Eu}(\mathbf{TTA})@\mathbf{MUM5}\).

