# 2.2. Experimental equipment

Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) measurements were collected under the 2θ range from 5 to \(50^{\circ}\) on a Bruker D8-Advance diffractometer. Energy-dispersive X-ray spectra (EDS) were performed on a field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM, Hitachi S4800) with operating voltage at \(15~\mathrm{kV}\). Fluorescence spectra were investigated by Edinburgh FLS920 spectrophotometer with a \(450~\mathrm{W}\) xenon lamp as an excitation source. We carried luminescence lifetime measurements on a FLS920 steady-state and time-resolved fluorescence spectrometer (Edinburgh Instruments) at room temperature. The \(p H\) values of aqueous solutions were determined by an INESA PHS-25 pH meter with an E-201F pH composite electrode, which was carefully calibrated by standard buffer solution before testing. X-ray photoelectron (XPS) spectra were recorded by an Axis Ultra DLD spectrometer (Kratos). Fourier transform infrared spectra (FT-IR) were recorded on a Nicolet IS10 infrared spectrum radiometer from 4000 to \(400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) using ATR annex. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) were performed on a NETZSCH STA 409 thermal analyzer under air between 30 and \(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with a heating rate of \(15\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\). The color coordinates were calculated based on the international GE 1931 standards given by the Commission International de l'Eclairage (CIE). The UV–Vis absorption spectrums were got from a Shimadzu UV-1800 spectrometer

# 2.3. Preparation of UiO-66 \((P M A)_{x}[B D C{-}2O H]_{y},\,x+y=1)\)

UiO-66 \((\mathrm{[PMA]_{x}[B D C-2O H]_{y}},\mathrm{x+y=1})\) were synthesized according to the relevant works [38] with slight adjustments. We abbreviated the name of this material as MUM1–9, where the number "1–9" represents the percentage of PMA. Typically, \(\mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2}\cdot8\mathrm{H}_{2}0\) \((0.3223\ \mathrm{g},\ 1\ \mathrm{mmol})\), PMA \(\left(\mathbf{x}\ \mathbf{mmol}\right)\), BDC-2OH (y mmol), benzoic acid \((2.445\ \mathrm{g},\ 20\ \mathrm{mmol})\), and concentrated HCl \((0.170\ \mathrm{mL})\) were mixed with DMF \((17\ \mathrm{mL})\) in a \(50~\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave, and then placed in an oven at \(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). After cooling to room temperature, the yellow-green powder was collected by centrifugation and rinsed with DMF \((1\times20\ \mathrm{mL})\) to wash out the residual reagents in the pores. The product was activated by soaking in anhydrous ethanol for \(24~\mathrm{h}\). Finally, the green tea color product was dried under a vacuum oven at \(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\)

# 2.4. Preparation of Eu@MUM1–9

\(34~\mathrm{mg}\) MUM1–9 was placed in a \(25~\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave, followed by adding \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) \(\mathrm{Eu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}\) acetonitrile solution \((10\ \mathrm{mmol/L})\). Next, added \(5~\mathrm{mL}\) acetonitrile into the mixture and then placed the autoclave in an oven at \(65\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). The product was collected by centrifugation and rinsed with acetonitrile to remove residual \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions physically adsorbed on the surface. Finally, the product was placed in a vacuum oven for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).

# 2.5. Preparation of Eu(TTA)@MUM5

The as-prepared Eu@MUM5 \((68~\mathrm{mg})\) was placed in a \(50~\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave, followed by adding \(3\,\textrm{m L}\) TTA acetonitrile solution \((50\;\mathrm{mmol/L})\), \(3\;\mathrm{mL}\) triethylamine acetonitrile solution \((50\;\mathrm{mmol/L})\), and acetonitrile \((24\;\mathrm{mL})\). After \(24~\mathrm{h}\) of heating at \(65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the obtained mixture was centrifugated and washed with acetonitrile. Finally, the product was dried under vacuum at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).

washed with acetonitrile. Finally, the product was dried under vacuum at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) .

