# 3. Results and discussion

# 3.1. Synthesis and characterization

To introduce \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions into UiO-66-2OH successfully, we use PMA to replace the original ligand of UiO-66-2OH through a ligand-exchange strategy (Fig. 1(a)). Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the as-synthesized MUM1–9 (Fig. 1(b)) are in good agreement with the reported one [39]. As the loading levels of PMA increased, the crystallinity of MUM1–9 shows a gradual decline trend. The PMA here serves as not only an acceptor of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions but also acts as an antenna to sensitize the luminescence of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions. From Fig. 2(a), the powder XRD patterns of Eu@MUM1–9 are consistent with UiO-66-2OH, which proves that the coordination post-synthetic method is nondestructive.

However, because of the limited sensitizing effect of PMA, the emission of Eu@MUM1–9 at \(613~\mathrm{nm}\) is quite weak. Thus, we introduced TTA into the frameworks to further sensitize \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions. As shown in Fig. S1, the PXRD pattern of Eu(TTA)@MUM1–9 matches well with UiO-66-2OH, which indicates the successful synthesis of Eu(TTA)@MUM1–9 and thus providing the basis for sensing research. The 0 1 s XPS spectra of MUM5 and Eu@MUM5 are performed in Fig. S2, the binding energy of 0 1 s in Eu@MUM5 is \(530.28\ \mathrm{eV}\), which is higher than that in MUM5. This change can be ascribed to the coordination of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions and the free carboxyl group on MUM5. Besides, we performed EDS element analysis on MUM5, Eu@MUM5, Eu(TTA)@MUM5 (Fig. S3) and listed their elements percentage in Table. S1. The weight percentage of Eu element in Eu@MUM5 and Eu(TTA)@MUM5 is 4.46 and 2.91, respectively. The reduce of Eu element's weight percentage is due to the increase in average molecular weight and the

