The FT-IR spectra of MUM1–9 are shown in Fig. S4, which is consistent with the previous report [40]. The absorption band at around \(570~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is ascribed to the stretch vibration of \(\mathrm{Zr-O-C}\) in the framework. Except for MUM1, all MUM3–9 shows a strong absorption peak at \(1238~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), which is attributed to the \(\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{O}\) stretching of the phenolic hydroxyl group on the aromatic ring [41]. The absorption peak at 1587 and \(1400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are ascribed to the asymmetric and symmetric stretches of the \(\mathrm{COO}^{-}\)groups from the ligand. The broad absorption peak at around \(3260~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is originated from the \(\mathrm{O-H}\) vibration in the frameworks.

The as-prepared UiO-66-2OH, MUM5, Eu@MUM5 were also monitored by TG analysis (Fig. S5). The TGA patterns of all these samples have a slight weight loss at around \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which is the removal of solvents like acetonitrile and ethanol in the MOFs channel. The initial decomposition temperature of the organic ligand in UiO-66-2OH is around \(240~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Interestingly, after introducing PMA, the decomposition temperature of MUM5 raises to \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which is similar to the previous report [42]. The weight loss percentage of MUM5, Eu@MUM5, and UiO-66-2OH is \(40.38\%\), \(37.33\%\), and \(40.37\%\), respectively. The decreasing of weight loss percentage of Eu@MUM5 is attributed to the doped \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions in the frameworks.

The photoluminescence spectra of PMA, BDC-2OH, MUM1-9 and Eu@MUM1-9 are examined at room temperature. As shown in Fig. S6, BDC-2OH has an excitation peak at \(388~\mathrm{nm}\) and exhibits a wide emission band from 430 to \(660~\mathrm{nm}\) with a peak at around \(490~\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. S7). The PMA does not show any luminescent property at the range of \(380{-}750~\mathrm{nm}\) because of the strong electron attracting effect of the carboxyl group. After introducing the \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions into the MUM1–9 framework, only Eu@MUM5, Eu@MUM7, and Eu@MUM9 show slight characteristic emission of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ion in solid-state (Fig. 2(b)).

Unfortunately, the emission of Eu@MUM1–9 becomes weaker after dissolving in water than that in solid phase, which is due to the quenching effect of \(\mathrm{O-H}\) vibration originating from water [43–45]. To enhance the fluorescent performance, the sensitizer TTA is introduced by applying post-synthetic modification on Eu@MUM1–9. The result is unsatisfactory because the sensitization of TTA is too efficient that all the products exhibit strong red emission, for example, the emission intensity at \(613~\mathrm{nm}\) of Eu(ITA)@MUM5 is 61 times higher than Eu@MUM5 (Fig. S8). For sake of both strong emission at 530 and \(613~\mathrm{nm}\), we proposed a "score criterion". First, the emission spectra of Eu@MUM1–9 in \(\mathrm{pH}=12\) aqueous solution are tested (Fig. 2(c)), and the emission intensity at \(530~\mathrm{nm}\) is normalized as \(I_{530\mathrm{nm}}\). Second, the contribution of \(613~\mathrm{nm}\) is evaluated by the luminescence of solid-state Eu@MUM1–9 and the emission intensity at \(613~\mathrm{nm}\) is normalized as \(I_{613\mathrm{nm}}\). Third, to balance the contribution of numerical emission intensity at \(613~\mathrm{nm}\) and \(530~\mathrm{nm}\), the emission spectra of Eu(TTA)@MUM5 in \(\mathrm{pH}=7\) and \(\mathrm{pH}=12\) aqueous solution are obtained (Fig. S9), where the ratio of intensity at 613 (\(\mathrm{pH}=7\)) and \(530~\mathrm{nm}\) (\(\mathrm{pH}=12\)) is about 3. Finally, the scores of MUM1–9 are calculated by [Score] = \(3^{-4}\) [I530nm] + [I613nm] and the result is shown in Fig. 2 (d), where MUM5 gets the highest score. After that, the MUM5 is used to further functionalize with \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions.

As illustrated in Fig. S10, we investigated the influence of loading levels of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions in Eu(TTA)@MUM5 on the emission intensity, where the amount of TTA is 3 times of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions. The result reveals that the optimal molar ratio of the free carboxyl group and \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions is 4:1. To explain this result, we tested the PXRD patterns of Eu@MUM5 with different concentrations of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions. As shown in Fig. S11, with the increasing of the \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions loading, the crystallinity of Eu@MUM5 reduced. The FT-IR spectrum (Fig. S12) reveals that the \(\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{O}\) vibration of the phenolic hydroxyl group on the aromatic ring becomes much weaker as the concentration of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions increased. The quenching of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions in high concentrations is due to the destroyed structure of
