# 3.5. Possible sensing mechanism

To explore the \(p\mathrm{H}\) sensing mechanism of Eu(TTA)@MUM5, we first studied the structural stability of the framework. The powder of Eu(TTA)@MUM5 was soaked into acid \((p\mathrm{H}=1.87)\) and alkaline \((p\mathrm{H}=10.65)\) solution for \(24\ \mathrm{h}\) and then be collected by centrifugation and dried at \(80\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under vacuum. As shown in Fig. S20, the probe keeps crystallinity well, which means that the luminescent response is not due to the structure change. In the acid or weak alkaline region, the emission of ligand BDC-2OH \((530\ \mathrm{nm})\) almost unchanged while the emission from \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions \((613\ \mathrm{nm})\) quenched gradually as the \(p\mathrm{H}\) of the solution become smaller, thus, we just need to study the quenching mechanism of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions when \(p\mathrm{H}<9.65\). As mentioned before, the emission of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions are from the sensitization of its coordination ligands. Thus, we calculated the lowest triplet energy of PMA, BDC-2OH, and TTA using the density functional theory (DFT) method at the \(\mathrm{B3LYP}/6-31+\mathrm{G}(\mathrm{d},\mathrm{p})\) level. As shown in Table. S2, the lowest triplet energy of BDC-2OH is \(17634\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), which is only \(134\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) above the acceptable level of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions \((^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\ 17500\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})\), such a minus energy gap is impossible to transfer energy from BDC-2OH to \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions. The lowest triplet energy of PMA is \(21747\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), which is higher than the \(^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\) energy level of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions by \(4247\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\). This energy gap could transfer energy to \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions, which is consistent with the emission spectrum of solid Eu@MUM5. But such an energy gap is too high to transfer energy effectively [52]. The lowest triplet energy of TTA is calculated as \(20221\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), the energy gap is \(2721\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) which matches well with \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions [53]. Thus, the emission of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions are not quenched by the protonation of its surrounded ligands. However, as shown in Fig. 8(a), the protonation of the PMA made it hard to chelate \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions tightly. Similar to PMA, the strong acid solution would affect the coordination ability of TTA and \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions through protonation as well [47]. As a result, \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions and TTA molecular gradually dissolved into the solvent and the energy transfer path is cut off, which causes the instantaneous declination of Eu(TTA)@MUM5's emission. The \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{H}\) vibration of water molecules could also quench the emission of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions, which act as an accelerator to the quenching of luminescent.

In the alkaline solution \((p\mathrm{H}=10.35-13.38)\), the emission intensity at \(613\ \mathrm{nm}\) reduced significantly, while the emission at \(530\ \mathrm{nm}\) enhanced from \(p\mathrm{H}=10.35\) to \(11.08\) and then quenched gradually. As the crystal structure of Eu(TTA)@MUM5 is destroyed in the

